    {
      "short": "Add an index",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "title": "Indexing — O(n) scans vs O(log n) lookups",
      "body": [
        "Without an <span class=\"term\">index</span>, the database scans every row for every query — linear cost. An index is a sorted lookup structure (usually a <span class=\"term\">B-tree</span>): it finds rows in O(log n).",
        "The 'tuned indexes' option turned 5-second scans back into 5-millisecond lookups."
      ],
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Small test data hides O(n) scans. This failure mode is invisible in dev and brutal in prod."
      },
